{
  "title": "Classes and __init__",
  "summary": "Define types with state and behaviour.",
  "minutes": 10,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "A class",
      "body": [
        "```python",
        "class Account:",
        "    def __init__(self, owner, balance=0):",
        "        self.owner = owner          # attributes are created by assigning",
        "        self.balance = balance",
        "",
        "    def deposit(self, amount):",
        "        self.balance += amount",
        "        return self.balance",
        "",
        "acc = Account(\"ann\")     # no `new`",
        "acc.deposit(50)",
        "print(acc.balance)       # 50",
        "```",
        "",
        "- `__init__` is the constructor. There are no field declarations: assigning `self.x` creates the attribute.",
        "- Every method takes the instance explicitly as its first parameter, called `self` by convention. C#'s `this` is implicit; Python's is not."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "class Counter:",
        "    def __init__(self, start=0):",
        "        self.value = start",
        "",
        "    def bump(self, by=1):",
        "        self.value += by",
        "        return self",
        "",
        "c = Counter(10)",
        "c.bump().bump(5)",
        "d = Counter()",
        "print(c.value, d.value)"
      ],
      "answer": "16 0",
      "explain": "Returning `self` lets calls chain. Each instance has its own `value`."
    },
    {
      "type": "mcq",
      "prompt": "What is wrong with this method?",
      "code": [
        "class Point:",
        "    def __init__(self, x):",
        "        self.x = x",
        "    def move(dx):",
        "        self.x += dx"
      ],
      "options": [
        "It forgot `self`, so calling `p.move(1)` raises `TypeError`",
        "Methods cannot be called `move`",
        "`x` must be declared first",
        "Nothing"
      ],
      "answer": 0,
      "explain": "Python passes the instance as the first argument. Without a `self` parameter, `p.move(1)` passes two arguments to a one-parameter function."
    },
    {
      "type": "learn",
      "title": "Class attributes and privacy",
      "body": [
        "Attributes assigned in the class body belong to the **class**, shared by every instance, like C# `static`:",
        "",
        "```python",
        "class Dog:",
        "    species = \"canis familiaris\"   # shared",
        "    def __init__(self, name):",
        "        self.name = name           # per instance",
        "```",
        "",
        "Python has no `private`. By convention:",
        "",
        "- `_balance` (one underscore) means \"internal, please do not touch\".",
        "- `__balance` (two) triggers name-mangling to `_Account__balance`, to avoid clashes in subclasses. Use it rarely.",
        "",
        "\"We are all consenting adults here\" is the Python attitude."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "predict",
      "prompt": "What does this print?",
      "code": [
        "class Dog:",
        "    tricks = []                  # shared by ALL dogs: a bug",
        "    def __init__(self, name):",
        "        self.name = name",
        "    def learn(self, trick):",
        "        self.tricks.append(trick)",
        "",
        "a, b = Dog(\"rex\"), Dog(\"fido\")",
        "a.learn(\"sit\")",
        "print(b.tricks)"
      ],
      "answer": "['sit']",
      "explain": "The mutable-default trap again, at class level. `tricks` belongs to the class, so every dog shares one list. Create it in `__init__` instead."
    },
